            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Approaching the presence of God, restoration, and returning from exile. In biblical spatial symbolism, moving west contrasts with eastward exile, representing a return to God's dwelling place.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 3:24\",\"Exodus 27:13-18\",\"Ezekiel 43:1-4\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Complete removal, infinite distance, or totality (especially concerning the forgiveness of sins when paired with 'East').\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 103:12\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"},{\"meaning\":\"The sea, the Gentile nations, or universal ingathering (derived from the Mediterranean Sea forming Israel's western border, and the Hebrew word 'yam' meaning both 'sea' and 'west').\",\"basis\":[\"Hosea 11:10\",\"Matthew 8:11\",\"Luke 13:29\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Finality, the close of an era, or darkness (associated with the setting sun).\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 50:1\",\"Isaiah 45:6\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolic meaning of 'west' is highly context-dependent. In biblical theology, its most profound symbolism relates to the Tabernacle and Temple, which were oriented facing east; thus, entering and moving toward the Holy of Holies required moving west. Geographically and linguistically, however, 'west' is synonymous with the Mediterranean Sea ('yam' in Hebrew), often representing the Gentile nations or the ends of the earth.\"}",
